\label{ch:garbage-collection}

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:gc-intro}

Garbage collection (GC) is the automatic reclamation of memory that is no longer accessible to the program. Instead of requiring the programmer to explicitly free memory, the garbage collector periodically identifies and reclaims \emph{garbage}---objects that can no longer affect the future computation. This abstraction eliminates entire classes of bugs (dangling pointers, double-free, memory leaks) at the cost of runtime overhead and reduced control over when memory is reclaimed.

Garbage collection is one of the most studied topics in computer science, with a literature spanning six decades. This chapter covers the fundamental algorithms, from the classic mark-and-sweep and copying collectors to modern generational, concurrent, and incremental approaches.

\section{Reachability and Roots}\label{sec:reachability}

\subsection{Defining Garbage}\label{sec:defining-garbage}

The theoretical foundation of garbage collection is the concept of \emph{reachability}.

\begin{definition}[Reachability]\label{def:reachability}
An object $o$ is \emph{reachable} if there exists a path from a \emph{root} to $o$ through a chain of references. The \emph{roots} are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item References in CPU registers.
  \item References on the call stack (local variables, parameters).
  \item References in global/static variables.
  \item References in handles managed by the runtime (e.g., JNI global references).
\end{itemize}
An object that is not reachable is \emph{garbage} and may be reclaimed.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Reachable Set]\label{def:reachable-set}
The \emph{reachable set} $\mathcal{R}$ is defined inductively:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item All root objects are in $\mathcal{R}$.
  \item If $o \in \mathcal{R}$ and $o$ contains a reference to $o'$, then $o' \in \mathcal{R}$.
\end{enumerate}
The garbage set is $\mathcal{G} = \mathcal{H} \setminus \mathcal{R}$, where $\mathcal{H}$ is the set of all allocated objects.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Conservative vs.\ Precise Collection}\label{sec:conservative-precise}

\begin{definition}[Conservative GC]\label{def:conservative-gc}
A \emph{conservative} garbage collector treats any value that \emph{looks like} a pointer as a pointer. This is necessary when the language does not distinguish pointers from integers (as in C/C++) or when stack scanning cannot precisely identify pointer locations.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Precise GC]\label{def:precise-gc}
A \emph{precise} (or \emph{exact}) garbage collector knows exactly which values are pointers and which are not, using type information or stack maps.
\end{definition}

Precise collectors can move objects (updating all references), while conservative collectors cannot (because they might miss interior pointers or misidentify integers as pointers).

